# QUIZ5: balancing questions to bring thin modules (0,3,6,12,14,15,18) up to 9 each.
QUIZ5 = {0: [{'q': 'A sensor, a PLC, and a motor starter together form what basic control building block?', 'options': ['A network switch', 'An input → decision → output control loop', 'A safety relay', 'A SCADA server'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Automation at its core is sense (input) → decide (controller logic) → act (output). Everything scales up from that loop.'}, {'q': 'Which document set does a tech reach for FIRST when troubleshooting an unfamiliar machine?', 'options': ['The vendor sales brochure', 'The electrical schematics / prints', 'The purchase order', 'The company handbook'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Electrical prints (schematics, wiring diagrams) are the primary reference for troubleshooting any system you did not install yourself. They show: power distribution (which breaker feeds what), I/O addresses (which PLC input is connected to which sensor), safety circuit paths (E-stop chain, guard monitoring), and interconnections between panels and field devices. The first thing a skilled tech does on an unfamiliar machine is locate the prints and orient to the power distribution before touching anything. When prints are wrong or missing, update or create them as part of the repair process.'}, {'q': '"Mean Time To Repair" is driven down most by which AET skill?', 'options': ['Faster typing', 'Systematic troubleshooting and print literacy', 'Buying more spares', 'Working longer shifts'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'MTTR (Mean Time To Repair) is driven by the time to: (1) detect the fault, (2) diagnose the root cause, (3) obtain the correct part, (4) physically repair and test. Diagnostic skill most impacts step 2: a tech who can read a P&ID, follow a wiring diagram, and use a DMM systematically localizes most faults in 15-30 minutes. Compare to a tech who swaps parts randomly -- they may replace 3 wrong components before finding the issue, adding 2-4 hours and creating new problems. Investing in technician training and keeping accurate documentation directly reduces MTTR and improves plant availability.'}], 3: [{'q': 'A CTD (count-down) counter reaches zero. Its done bit typically:', 'options': ['Stays off forever', 'Sets to signal the count is complete', 'Resets the PLC', 'Doubles the preset'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'CTU .DN sets when ACC &ge; PRE. CTD .DN sets when ACC &le; 0. Both are edge-triggered: each FALSE-to-TRUE rung transition increments/decrements ACC by 1, regardless of how long the rung stays TRUE. Reset with RES to clear .ACC and .DN. CTU use: count parts, trigger a batch stop or PM task when ACC = PRE. CTD use: count down from a recipe quantity, fire .DN when the lot is complete. Never share one counter between two functions -- use separate CTU instances.'}, {'q': 'You need a one-shot pulse that fires for exactly one scan on a rising edge. Which instruction?', 'options': ['TON', 'ONS (one-shot)', 'LATCH', 'MOV'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'ONS (One-Shot / OSR in ControlLogix): when its input transitions FALSE-to-TRUE, the output is TRUE for exactly one program scan, then FALSE for all subsequent scans even if the input remains TRUE. This converts a held signal (pushbutton held down) into a single event. Application: use OSR before a CTU counter to prevent a held button from incrementing the count every scan. Use OSF (One-Shot Falling) for detecting the trailing edge of a signal. Store tag must be unique per OSR instruction -- sharing store tags causes double-triggering artifacts.'}, {'q': 'An RTO (retentive timer) differs from a TON because it:', 'options': ['Runs faster', 'Keeps its accumulated value when the rung goes false', 'Cannot be reset', 'Only counts down'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'RTO (Retentive Timer ON Delay): .ACC accumulates total on-time across multiple enable/disable cycles. Unlike TON (.ACC resets on rung-false), RTO retains .ACC until an explicit RES instruction clears it. Use case: track cumulative motor runtime for PM scheduling. Motor runs 2.5 h Monday and 3.0 h Tuesday -- RTO totals 5.5 h. When .ACC &ge; PRE (e.g., 500 h), .DN fires and generates a work order. Store .ACC in retained (non-volatile) memory tags so power cycles do not reset the PM counter.'}], 6: [{'q': 'An FRL unit in a pneumatic system provides:', 'options': ['Filter, Regulator, Lubricator', 'Flow, Return, Load', 'Fast Reset Latch', 'Fault Relay Logic'], 'answer': 0, 'explain': 'FRL unit (Filter-Regulator-Lubricator): (1) Filter -- removes water, rust particles, and debris from plant air supply; must be drained regularly (auto-drain preferred). (2) Regulator -- sets the downstream pressure for the circuit (typically 4-6 bar for cylinders); maintains stable pressure despite supply fluctuations. (3) Lubricator -- injects a fine oil mist into the air stream to lubricate sliding seals in valves and cylinders. Some modern pneumatic components are oil-free (do not use a lubricator with them -- it contaminates the components). Size FRL for the circuit flow rate plus 20% reserve.'}, {'q': 'Doubling a hydraulic cylinder bore area roughly does what to output force at the same pressure?', 'options': ['Halves it', 'Leaves it unchanged', 'Doubles it', 'Squares it'], 'answer': 2, 'explain': 'Force = Pressure × Area (F = P × A). A cylinder with a 2-inch bore has an area of π × 1² ≈ 3.14 in². At 100 PSI: F = 100 × 3.14 = 314 lbf. Double the bore to 4 inches (radius = 2): area = π × 4 = 12.57 in², so F = 1,257 lbf &mdash; four times more force (area scales as r²). Rod-side force is less because the rod cross-section reduces the effective area. This relationship explains why small-bore cylinders need higher pressure for the same clamping force.'}, {'q': 'A pneumatic cylinder drifts back after stopping mid-stroke. The most likely cause is:', 'options': ['Air is compressible, so it cannot hold position rigidly', 'The PLC crashed', 'Low voltage', 'A blown fuse'], 'answer': 0, 'explain': 'Because air compresses, pneumatics cannot hold an intermediate position stiffly — hydraulics or a lock is needed for that.'}], 12: [{'q': 'During commissioning you bump-test a motor. What are you verifying?', 'options': ['Its color', 'Correct rotation direction before coupling the load', 'The paint finish', 'The nameplate font'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Bump testing (jog test) procedure: momentarily jog the motor for 0.5-1 second and observe rotation direction before allowing it to run fully. Reason: three-phase motor rotation is set by phase sequence -- swapping any two of three leads reverses rotation. Running a centrifugal pump backward causes cavitation and may unseat the impeller. A backward conveyor can dump or jam product. After verifying correct rotation, also verify that current is within nameplate FLA at no-load, and that vibration and sound are normal before loading.'}, {'q': 'A punch list at project handover is:', 'options': ['A list of employees', 'Outstanding items to fix before final acceptance', 'The lunch schedule', 'A parts catalog'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'A punch list (snagging list) is created during the Final Acceptance Test (FAT/SAT): the customer and integrator walk the system together and document every deficiency -- a label missing, a tag not in the documentation, an alarm threshold that needs adjustment. The punch list is contractually significant: the integrator must resolve all critical items before the customer signs off and triggers final payment. Items are classified: Critical (blocks sign-off), Major (must be resolved within 30 days), Minor (nice-to-have, may be deferred). Keep punch lists in writing -- never verbal.'}, {'q': 'System integration means:', 'options': ['Installing one PLC', 'Making sensors, drives, PLCs, HMI, safety, and networks work together as one coordinated machine', 'Only wiring power', 'Writing a manual'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Integration is the discipline of tying every subsystem -- electrical, mechanical, fluid power, controls, networks -- into a reliable and coordinated whole. An integrator bridges the gap between individual vendor components (PLC from Rockwell, drives from ABB, SCADA from Ignition) and a working system. Faults in integrated systems hide at the seams between subsystems; the integration mindset means following the signal chain across every boundary until the root cause is found.'}, {'q': 'The best defense against a chaotic startup is:', 'options': ['Skipping documentation', 'A written commissioning checklist executed in order', 'Powering everything at once', 'Guessing'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Commissioning sequence (bottom-up): (1) Power stage: verify all supply voltages, wiring continuity, and terminations. (2) I/O stage: force each output, physically verify the actuator responds, confirm each input reads correctly in the PLC I/O tree. (3) Safety stage: test each safety function (E-stop, interlocks, light curtain) independently. (4) Dry run: run the sequence without product to verify motion paths and timing. (5) Loaded run: run at slow speed with product, then full speed. Each stage must pass before advancing to the next.'}], 14: [{'q': 'In an edge/fog/cloud architecture, the CLOUD layer is best suited for:', 'options': ['Microsecond motion control', 'Long-term analytics, fleet dashboards, and machine learning', 'Directly switching a contactor', 'Reading a limit switch'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Edge-fog-cloud architecture: Edge (PLC, embedded controller, edge PC) handles real-time control in microseconds to milliseconds -- latency is deterministic and locally contained. Fog layer (on-premise server, industrial PC) handles site aggregation, local HMI, short-term historian, and local ML inference in seconds. Cloud layer (AWS, Azure, GCP) handles long-term data storage, cross-site analytics, model training, and business intelligence in minutes to hours. Latency increases at each layer -- never put a time-critical safety or control function above the edge layer.'}, {'q': 'An MQTT broker’s job is to:', 'options': ['Generate electricity', 'Route published messages to subscribed clients by topic', 'Replace the PLC', 'Provide 480V power'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'The MQTT broker is the server that receives ALL published messages and routes them to ALL matching subscribers. Publisher sends to a topic (plant/line1/motor/temp), never knowing who is subscribed. Subscribers register interest in topics (using wildcards: # = all, + = one level) and receive all matching messages. The broker stores retained messages so late-connecting subscribers get the last known value. Common brokers: Mosquitto (open source), HiveMQ, AWS IoT Core. The decoupled design means you can add a new analytics subscriber without changing any publisher or the PLC.'}, {'q': 'A "digital twin" is:', 'options': ['A backup hard drive', 'A live virtual model of a physical asset fed by real data', 'A second operator', 'A spare PLC'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'A digital twin is a live virtual model of a physical asset that receives real sensor data and mirrors the asset state in near real-time. Uses: (1) Simulation: test control logic changes, PLC programs, or setpoint adjustments in the twin before applying to the real machine. (2) Monitoring: compare predicted vs. actual behavior -- deviations indicate developing faults. (3) Predictive maintenance: ML models on twin data predict remaining useful life of bearings, motors, or tools. (4) Training: operators practice fault scenarios on the twin without risk to the real system. Tools: Siemens Plant Simulation, Rockwell Emulate3D, NVIDIA Omniverse.'}], 15: [{'q': 'When using a clamp meter to read motor current without breaking the circuit, you clamp around:', 'options': ['All three conductors at once', 'A single conductor', 'The ground wire only', 'The conduit'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Clamp meters measure current via magnetic field around a conductor. Clamping all conductors (hot + neutral, or all three phases) together: opposing current directions cancel the magnetic fields and the meter reads zero. To measure: clamp only ONE conductor at a time. For three-phase motors: clamp one phase at a time and compare all three -- a significant imbalance (&gt;5%) indicates a winding problem, single-phasing, or unbalanced supply. Exception: clamp all three phases together to detect ground faults -- any net reading means current is returning via earth, not the supply.'}, {'q': 'A "voltage present but no current flow" reading on a de-energized load usually indicates:', 'options': ['A healthy circuit', 'An open load or downstream open (the circuit is broken)', 'A short circuit', 'Correct operation'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Full supply voltage at a terminal with zero current = open circuit downstream. Voltage appears because no current flows through the source impedance, so no voltage drop occurs before the measurement point. The open has infinite resistance and takes all the voltage by KVL. Trace downstream node by node: the first node where voltage drops to 0 V is just past the open. Common causes: blown fuse, open load coil, broken wire, corroded terminal, open contact. This is the same principle whether in a 480 V motor circuit or a 24 VDC control circuit.'}, {'q': 'Before trusting any "dead" reading during LOTO, best practice is to:', 'options': ['Assume it is off', 'Verify the meter on a known-live source before and after (live-dead-live)', 'Skip the meter', 'Ask a coworker'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Live-dead-live verification: (1) Test the meter on a KNOWN live voltage (prove it works and the leads are intact). (2) Test the circuit you are about to work on (confirm it reads dead). (3) Test the known live source again (prove the meter still works after the dead test). This three-step check catches a dead battery, blown input fuse, or bad probe connection that would cause a live circuit to falsely read zero. NFPA 70E and OSHA 1910.333 treat live-dead-live as best practice for all energized electrical work.'}], 18: [{'q': 'A certification roadmap should be prioritized primarily by:', 'options': ['Which has the coolest logo', 'Return on investment for your career path', 'Alphabetical order', 'Which is cheapest only'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Choose certifications by ROI: does this credential appear in job postings for roles you want? Does it pay back the study and exam cost in salary premium within 1-2 years? ISA CCST is the clearest AET credential ROI -- it is listed in real job postings. A certification that impresses peers but is not recognized by hiring managers or reflected in pay is a vanity credential. Best sequence: ISA CCST I (entry), CCST II (after 5 years), then vendor credentials that match your site equipment.'}, {'q': 'In a behavioral interview, the STAR method structures answers as:', 'options': ['Start, Talk, Argue, Rest', 'Situation, Task, Action, Result', 'Safety, Tools, Access, Report', 'Study, Test, Apply, Renew'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'STAR behavioral interview method: Situation = specific context (When, Where). Task = what you were responsible for achieving. Action = specifically what YOU did (use I, not we). Result = the measurable outcome (quantify: saved 2 hours, reduced faults by 30%, zero recordables). STAR answers are concrete and verifiable; they demonstrate competence through evidence rather than claims. Interviewers remember STAR answers because they tell a story. Prepare 5-6 STAR stories covering: troubleshooting, teamwork, safety, initiative, and handling a mistake.'}, {'q': 'The strongest signal of hands-on competence in a portfolio is:', 'options': ['A long list of buzzwords', 'Documented projects: a wiring photo, a ladder program, a before/after fix', 'A fancy font', 'Number of pages'], 'answer': 1, 'explain': 'Portfolio evidence hierarchy: (1) Artifacts (photos of panels you built, programs you wrote with brief explanations, documented troubleshooting sequences with root-cause findings) -- highest value; shows actual work. (2) Metrics (reduced downtime by 25%, designed a PM program that eliminated 3 recurring failures) -- quantified impact. (3) Credentials (ISA CCST, Rockwell cert) -- validates knowledge base. (4) Soft claims (hard worker, good with PLC) -- lowest value; unverifiable. Build your portfolio by documenting real work as you complete it -- not by trying to reconstruct it later.'}]}
